            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Vespasian</strong><br>\nVespasian was born as Titus Flavius Vespasianus on November 17, 9 AD, in a village north-east of Rome. He came from a family that had a history of military service. Vespasian began his own military career as a legionnaire and quickly rose through the ranks. His early career included a mix of military commands and political appointments, which were typical paths to prominence in the Roman Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nVespasian was not born into a particularly distinguished or influential family, but he became a respected commander. He served with distinction in the Roman invasion of Britain in 43 AD and quelled the Jewish rebellion in Judea. His successes in Judea, coupled with the instability of Emperor Nero’s rule, set the stage for his rise to emperor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Year of the Four Emperors</strong><br>\nThe year 69 AD was known as the \"Year of the Four Emperors.\" After Nero's suicide, Galba, Otho, and Vitellius each took the title of emperor in quick succession, but none held it securely. Vespasian, who was then in Egypt, was proclaimed emperor by his legions. He returned to Rome and, after a brief civil war, secured the throne, ending the year's turmoil.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
