            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Reforms and Policies</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Vespasian was known for his fiscal reforms. He restored the state’s finances through careful management and various measures, including new taxes. He was pragmatic, reducing the corruption that had become commonplace in Roman administration, and he focused on public works, including the construction of the Colosseum.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Works</strong><br>\nVespasian invested in large-scale public works to win the public's favor and provide employment. The most famous of these was the Colosseum, initially known as the Flavian Amphitheatre, which he began in 70 AD. This massive structure was funded by the spoils from the Jewish Temple after the Siege of Jerusalem.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Achievements</strong><br>\nMilitary accomplishments marked Vespasian’s reign. He maintained peace in the empire's territories and expanded control in Britain. His leadership during the Jewish Revolt was notable for his strategic acumen and resulted in the destruction of the Temple in Jerusalem, signaling Rome’s dominance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
