          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relations with the Senate</strong><br>\nVespasian managed to maintain a good relationship with the Roman Senate, which was not always easy for emperors. He respected senatorial privileges and sought to involve the Senate in government decisions. This approach was a departure from the previous emperor's policies and helped stabilize his rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Succession</strong><br>\nVespasian died of natural causes on June 23, 79 AD. He was succeeded by his two sons, Titus and Domitian, in turn. His last words were reportedly a joke about becoming a god, which reflected his well-known sense of humor. His death marked the end of the short-lived Flavian dynasty's founding, as his sons continued his lineage.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nVespasian’s legacy is marked by his return to traditional Roman virtues and his public building projects. He was seen as a ruler who brought stability after a period of chaos. His reign is often considered the beginning of the 'Flavian restoration' of the Roman Empire, setting a precedent for the emperors who followed.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
